cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Capability-as-Infrastructure: Software on Demand"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Shatny
    given-names: Michael
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-2011-3258"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23438908"  # concept DOI — always resolves to the latest version
repository-code: "https://github.com/semanticintent/capability-as-infrastructure"
abstract: >-
  Capability-as-Infrastructure (CaI) proposes that the durable unit of a software
  system is no longer the application but the declared capability, together with
  the data, history, decisions and authority it operates on. Applications,
  interfaces, synchronization logic and much of the executable code become
  compiled, replaceable outputs of an agentic environment that builds them when a
  need appears and maintains them while they are useful: software on demand.
  Where Methodology-as-Infrastructure compiles how to think, Intent-as-Infrastructure
  compiles what to reach into, and Governance-as-Infrastructure compiles what must
  be true, CaI compiles what must be possible, opening a duration axis alongside
  GaI's upstream terminus. Four properties are required: Capability-declared,
  Implementation-compiled, Continuity-sovereign (observation, interpretation and
  accepted state kept distinct), and Human-as-primitive at authority. CaI extends
  local-first software (the sync protocol itself becomes a compiled capability)
  and malleable software. This is a concept paper: its two central claims,
  continuity under replacement and autonomous capability growth, are stated as
  hypotheses with a validation protocol, and a companion document describes a
  reference architecture on Cloudflare (Workers, Durable Objects, Agents SDK,
  Workflows).
